   "evidence": "`def IsFiniteType` at TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/RootSystem/FiniteType/Basic.lean:121-123 conjoins `(\u2200 i, A i i = 2)`, `(\u2200 i j, i \u2260 j \u2192 A i j \u2264 0)` and the vanishing pattern with the symmetrizer clause; `isFiniteType_of` (line 141) correspondingly takes `h2` and `hle` as separate hypotheses. SimplyLaced.lean:14-15 itself says `IsFiniteType` \"says nothing directly about the matrix itself\".",
   "file": "TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/RootSystem/FiniteType/Basic.lean",
   "fix": "State the one direction that holds: with the constant-one vector, positive definiteness of `A.map Int.cast` supplies the symmetrization clause of `IsFiniteType`, the generalized-Cartan clauses being checked separately. Drop \"the same condition\"/\"exchanged\"/\"on the nose\".",
   "issue": "The docstring of `of_one_mul_intCast_eq_map` claims that for symmetric `A`, \"positive definiteness of `A.map Int.cast` and finite type are then the same condition\". That is false: `IsFiniteType` also demands `A i i = 2` and off-diagonal `\u2264 0` (line 122), so e.g. `!![1]` is symmetric and positive definite but not of finite type; and the converse (finite type \u27f9 `A.map Int.cast` positive definite) is nowhere proved here \u2014 it is precisely why each family re-derives positive definiteness from its coordinate model. Same overstatement at line 41 of the same file (\"how positive definiteness of such a matrix and its being of finite type are exchanged\") and at SimplyLaced.lean:15-17 (\"the constant-one vector is a symmetrizer ... so positive definiteness holds on the nose\", which reads as if it followed from `IsFiniteType`, contradicting the preceding sentence of that very paragraph).",
   "line": 127
  }
 ],
